00:41.640 --> 00:43.640
All right, hello and welcome to Camel Finance.

00:43.720 --> 00:46.380
I'm your boy Camel and this is the weekend's deep dive on the weekend.

00:46.480 --> 00:50.320
We'd like to come back and re-examine the base case hypothesis and check to see if things are still going,

00:50.520 --> 00:52.360
roughly speaking, according to plan.

00:52.500 --> 00:55.300
So we're going to run through this thing from the top as always.

00:55.600 --> 00:59.380
For the stock market, we have been calling since the lows for a big blow off top to all-term highs,

00:59.380 --> 01:03.020
then a global recession, a bear market, heading into some four-year cycle lows

01:03.020 --> 01:06.660
and have been calling for a short period of asset price deflation towards those lows.

01:06.960 --> 01:09.800
Fast forward to today and it looks a lot like we're still in a blow off top.

01:09.880 --> 01:14.180
It has gone longer than I expected it to go, but I'm not in the business of picking a date to go short.

01:14.280 --> 01:17.520
I would rather just follow the trend until it breaks and so far it hasn't broken.

01:17.900 --> 01:22.280
I actually think there is more probability of new highs here than a breakdown

01:22.280 --> 01:24.420
and we are extremely late in the four-year cycle.

01:24.420 --> 01:29.780
The four-year cycle is due late this year, but something tells me we have got a bit of gas left in the tank here

01:29.780 --> 01:31.420
to run this thing hotter for longer.

01:31.560 --> 01:35.320
So again, I'm not in the business of trying to be a top caller or the next Michael Burry.

01:35.440 --> 01:39.020
I am a trend follower and I'm going to continue to follow this trend until something goes bang.

01:39.240 --> 01:40.960
So far though, it hasn't really gone bang.

01:41.000 --> 01:45.660
And as you'll see when we get to the alarm signals, none of those are really alarming me just yet.

01:45.800 --> 01:48.400
It does seem a lot to me like we've completed this first bear trap

01:48.400 --> 01:51.440
and we are well and truly up in this FOMO euphoria neighborhood.

01:51.440 --> 01:54.960
So I'm not entirely sure whereabouts we are in here, but we have to be somewhere in here.

01:55.240 --> 01:59.920
I suppose there's a chance if the top is in, we have just passed euphoria and we'll soon put in a lower high.

02:00.100 --> 02:01.800
That would look something like this, I guess.

02:02.140 --> 02:04.220
But to me, it doesn't look like that's what's going to happen.

02:04.340 --> 02:06.520
It looks like we've probably got another one of these things first

02:06.520 --> 02:08.400
and then we can talk about that lower high coming in.

02:08.620 --> 02:09.820
So wild times for sure.

02:10.120 --> 02:14.440
For Bitcoin, the idea was that there are weekly cycles nested inside of its major four-year cycle.

02:14.820 --> 02:18.240
And whilst we didn't make any of the much higher numbers I was hoping to see this cycle,

02:18.240 --> 02:20.780
we have still well and truly obeyed a four-year cycle.

02:21.160 --> 02:24.140
The weekly cycles have been very, very consistent during this four-year cycle.

02:24.320 --> 02:29.820
And there's really only, as you can see here, a little bit more of this upside left to go out of the current weekly cycle.

02:29.960 --> 02:34.800
And then we should be looking for another left translated and failed weekly cycle to complete the four-year cycle

02:34.800 --> 02:38.360
where we plan to probably double the Bitcoin stack in here, I'm thinking,

02:38.600 --> 02:42.240
and likely add a bunch of higher beta assets like MicroStrategy and stuff like that.

02:42.240 --> 02:46.720
But so far, there's a whole heap of discussion and debate over whether the four-year cycle is real,

02:46.900 --> 02:51.060
what drives it, whether just saying you're going to blindly buy in October is crowded or not.

02:51.280 --> 02:54.720
But to that, I simply say, with respect, those people don't understand the cycle system.

02:54.820 --> 02:59.360
They don't understand how to nest the daily cycles inside of the weekly cycles and the weekly inside of the yearly.

02:59.740 --> 03:04.540
And if you understand that kind of thing, then you know that October doesn't even fit really from a cycle perspective.

03:04.680 --> 03:09.740
It actually makes a lot more sense to head down towards December or even early next year to complete that nesting.

03:09.740 --> 03:12.040
Right here, this is just a rough estimate.

03:12.180 --> 03:14.920
This says 29 weeks, but we've averaged 31 weeks.

03:14.960 --> 03:17.880
If we were to do the same, that would put the bottom around early January.

03:18.320 --> 03:20.980
So, so far, I'm just going to keep saying the cycles are in control.

03:21.100 --> 03:22.040
Everything else is narrative.

03:22.260 --> 03:24.860
I think this is well and truly following a four-year cycle,

03:24.980 --> 03:29.460
especially in the face of things like the NASDAQ and the stock market continuing to rip to new highs.

03:29.780 --> 03:32.700
So for a long time, people were talking about how this thing was just the NASDAQ

03:32.700 --> 03:35.340
and was just copying tech or was correlated to tech.

03:35.340 --> 03:39.800
But how do you explain since October it's been in a bear market and a four-year cycle decline

03:39.800 --> 03:42.020
whilst the stock market has just continued to run hot?

03:42.140 --> 03:44.200
So I think the cycle is well and truly in control.

03:44.320 --> 03:48.680
To be honest, I don't see any reasonable argument here to say there isn't a four-year cycle

03:48.680 --> 03:50.480
or the four-year cycle is not in control.

03:50.920 --> 03:55.060
I mean, the only component we are missing here is for a top to come in towards the end of August,

03:55.140 --> 03:58.880
if not before, and then roll over to new lows, and that will complete the four-year cycle.

03:59.200 --> 04:03.520
So by all means, feel free to reject any of the information presented on this channel at any time.

04:03.520 --> 04:05.260
But for me, this is still what's happening.

04:05.460 --> 04:08.800
And so we're going to continue to target that four-year cycle low for allocation

04:08.800 --> 04:11.520
to more Bitcoin and higher beaters, as I said earlier.

04:11.600 --> 04:15.180
As I also said earlier, we've been talking about seeing continued disinflation

04:15.180 --> 04:16.840
and a short period of asset price deflation,

04:16.840 --> 04:19.900
because anything you do up here shows up in the CPI with a lag.

04:20.260 --> 04:24.480
Now, my good friend E, who is from producing up-to-date charts for us,

04:24.700 --> 04:29.040
has pointed out that this actually looks a lot like we've already seen the bottom here,

04:29.080 --> 04:30.820
and we have started the resumption up.

04:30.820 --> 04:36.060
And I think at least according to the government-reported data, this seems to be the case.

04:36.160 --> 04:38.880
But I am still making the case, and again, feel free to reject this,

04:38.920 --> 04:40.200
that the government data is a lie.

04:40.300 --> 04:42.100
The government data is a heavily flawed basket,

04:42.460 --> 04:44.300
mostly because of the lagging shelter component.

04:44.580 --> 04:48.100
I'm still making the case that this is actually not a resumption of inflation

04:48.100 --> 04:49.560
about to continue to follow higher,

04:49.700 --> 04:53.740
and is very soon going to turn down and even slip negative, hence the deflation recall.

04:53.960 --> 04:55.680
I know this is extremely contested,

04:55.780 --> 04:58.480
and a lot of this is because we saw that spike in the oil price,

04:58.480 --> 05:03.720
which of course we did say would drag the CPI basket headline number up for a few months,

05:03.820 --> 05:04.660
which of course it did.

05:04.940 --> 05:08.080
But then we did manage to call the top here and suggest that something like this would be in play

05:08.080 --> 05:10.240
as oil declines for its four-year cycle low.

05:10.420 --> 05:12.300
And so far, this is pretty much what's happened.

05:12.440 --> 05:13.280
Okay, we had this spill.

05:13.380 --> 05:14.560
We've now put in the counter-trend bounce,

05:14.600 --> 05:16.120
as you'll see when we get to the charts at the end.

05:16.200 --> 05:19.180
And I still make the case that new lows are coming for oil.

05:19.380 --> 05:20.260
And if that is true,

05:20.260 --> 05:23.600
then we could only expect this disinflationary trend in inflation to continue.

05:23.600 --> 05:26.660
Again, as well, subtle tells, but the subtle tells are important.

05:26.820 --> 05:28.680
Another lower high and another lower low.

05:28.840 --> 05:30.840
So all that's missing here is new lows to follow.

05:31.000 --> 05:34.860
And this call remains intact despite all of the talk out there,

05:34.920 --> 05:37.300
despite all of the government headline prints,

05:37.300 --> 05:39.700
and despite the constant rhetoric of,

05:40.160 --> 05:42.740
we don't want inflation to tick up or inflation is sticky.

05:42.980 --> 05:45.840
Okay, when I look at the data sets that I monitor and track for trend,

05:46.080 --> 05:46.880
such as this,

05:47.200 --> 05:49.500
they are moving in the right direction for my call.

05:49.600 --> 05:52.220
So I'm going to continue to accept this until something breaks,

05:52.220 --> 05:53.520
but so far it hasn't broken.

05:53.660 --> 05:56.040
And we have been saying the same thing over and over again.

05:56.120 --> 05:57.260
And yet here we are.

05:57.300 --> 05:58.520
Okay, every one of these spikes,

05:58.560 --> 06:00.080
people have said that we're wrong, we're wrong, we're wrong.

06:00.140 --> 06:02.840
And here we get continuing to make lower highs and lower lows.

06:02.920 --> 06:04.760
So the trend is still disinflationary.

06:04.860 --> 06:08.640
And as expected after that wobble from the oil spike that we've seen,

06:08.680 --> 06:10.620
we have now started to pull back again.

06:10.620 --> 06:12.600
Okay, and head for what I believe is new lows.

06:12.680 --> 06:14.320
We are now continuing the disinflationary trend.

06:14.320 --> 06:17.100
We confirmed that this month with cold CPI data,

06:17.460 --> 06:19.840
the institutional traders have figured this out too,

06:19.840 --> 06:22.780
because the one-year inflation swaps have dropped below 2%,

06:22.780 --> 06:25.080
meaning people are a lot smarter and richer than us

06:25.080 --> 06:28.640
also seem to think inflation expectations over the next 12 months

06:28.640 --> 06:29.840
are going to be sub 2%,

06:29.840 --> 06:32.800
which again does not really align with the official narrative

06:32.800 --> 06:35.540
of inflation is sticky and persistent and a problem.

06:35.920 --> 06:39.140
Additionally, we saw the PCE data come in cooler than expected.

06:39.500 --> 06:43.040
And if you trim the mean year over year for the PCE data,

06:43.040 --> 06:43.760
you can see again,

06:43.840 --> 06:48.160
this is still undeniably a heavily disinflationary trend,

06:48.160 --> 06:51.540
heading for new lows and likely a short period of asset price deflation.

06:51.860 --> 06:53.340
Again, as soon as this changes,

06:53.440 --> 06:56.120
as soon as we start to print higher highs and higher lows on the chart,

06:56.180 --> 06:57.340
I will invalidate this call.

06:57.520 --> 07:00.320
Keep in mind, I have nothing to gain from calling this.

07:00.440 --> 07:01.700
Okay, I'm just calling it the way I see it.

07:01.740 --> 07:03.440
I'm not trading the inflation chart.

07:03.540 --> 07:06.140
So it's not like I'm short and don't want this thing to turn around

07:06.140 --> 07:07.640
and stop me out or anything like that.

07:07.700 --> 07:09.000
Okay, it's just a macro call.

07:09.100 --> 07:12.000
It's been my expectation that we would see this

07:12.000 --> 07:13.940
ever since we actually called the top in inflation.

07:14.060 --> 07:15.240
We did really well to nail that.

07:15.480 --> 07:17.120
And since then, I've been saying this would happen.

07:17.120 --> 07:19.280
And so I'm not trying to come here and say,

07:19.340 --> 07:22.500
I told you so or anything along those lines whatsoever.

07:22.500 --> 07:24.220
I'm simply pointing at this and saying,

07:24.300 --> 07:27.920
I had a call and that call is still in play until such time as it breaks.

07:27.960 --> 07:29.720
And as of right now, it has not broken.

07:30.020 --> 07:33.880
We've also been consistently calling for labour market deterioration.

07:34.040 --> 07:35.100
And that is what we've been seeing.

07:35.360 --> 07:36.720
There's plenty of metrics you can use,

07:37.040 --> 07:39.500
but I've been pointing at all of this and saying that at some point

07:39.500 --> 07:41.900
after we get this pull back into the four-year cycle low

07:41.900 --> 07:45.020
and what is likely to be quite a significant spill,

07:45.020 --> 07:48.800
I think similar to the C-19 plunge towards the end of this year,

07:49.080 --> 07:53.920
the NBER who's in charge of dating the recessions will likely go back after the fact,

07:54.220 --> 07:55.260
as they always do,

07:55.600 --> 07:58.040
and suggest that we were in recession for some time

07:58.040 --> 08:01.020
before we actually reached the bottom of the pullback

08:01.020 --> 08:02.560
and the four-year cycles for the stocks.

08:02.560 --> 08:05.980
And the simple truth of the matter is people love to jump up and down.

08:06.200 --> 08:08.280
And I say people, meaning really the narrative, right?

08:08.300 --> 08:10.940
The mainstream outlets, the government reported data and say,

08:11.000 --> 08:15.980
look, unemployment dropped for a high of 4.4 to 4.3 and now to 4.2%.

08:15.980 --> 08:17.740
But when we look at this, okay,

08:17.760 --> 08:20.420
this is still a trend of higher highs and higher lows.

08:20.500 --> 08:24.160
This still looks a lot like every other instance where we had a lead in before a pop.

08:24.280 --> 08:24.720
Okay.

08:24.740 --> 08:27.260
And each one of those pops was synonymous with a recession,

08:27.680 --> 08:29.500
hence the shaded bars over the chart.

08:29.500 --> 08:32.100
So if this ultimately resolved higher like this,

08:32.100 --> 08:34.780
and then the NBER go back and paint the bar over here somewhere,

08:35.120 --> 08:37.140
again, it won't be out of the ordinary.

08:37.340 --> 08:39.520
That won't be a crazy contrarian call.

08:39.640 --> 08:41.960
That will simply be an observation that yes,

08:42.000 --> 08:43.820
every single time this has happened historically,

08:43.820 --> 08:47.000
we got the spike, we got a recession, and that was that.

08:47.280 --> 08:48.100
So don't get me wrong.

08:48.160 --> 08:49.440
I am not cheering on unemployment.

08:49.640 --> 08:52.200
I am not cheering on people to lose their jobs and their livelihoods.

08:52.260 --> 08:54.840
I'm simply making an observation at the hard right edge of the data.

08:55.080 --> 08:56.520
I would love to see this, okay?

08:56.580 --> 08:58.460
Because I would obviously rather just say,

08:58.460 --> 08:59.400
well, I was wrong about that.

08:59.480 --> 09:01.760
We swerved a recession and we didn't get a spike in unemployment.

09:01.760 --> 09:03.660
I would rather say that than come back and say,

09:03.720 --> 09:06.800
I told you so, ha, everyone's lost their job and their homes, right?

09:07.020 --> 09:08.500
Obviously, I'm not a psychopath like that.

09:08.620 --> 09:10.620
But again, at the moment, to jump up and down and say,

09:10.660 --> 09:12.580
here we go, unemployment is dropping,

09:12.800 --> 09:14.180
the labour market's strong and resilient,

09:14.420 --> 09:15.520
the labour market's improving,

09:15.740 --> 09:17.220
we're re-accelerating in the economy.

09:17.360 --> 09:20.020
I still maintain that I just do not understand

09:20.020 --> 09:21.780
how people come to those types of conclusions.

09:21.980 --> 09:24.900
Okay, this just doesn't look anything like that to me at the moment.

09:24.900 --> 09:28.740
We can see the same trends in the 16 to 24-year-old unemployment rate.

09:28.740 --> 09:30.900
We can see the exact same trend in the U6.

09:31.140 --> 09:34.000
The Sam recession rule has started to roll over a little bit here.

09:34.120 --> 09:35.960
So maybe this is a leading indicator.

09:36.140 --> 09:37.600
Maybe this can continue to turn down.

09:37.980 --> 09:39.220
And then perhaps if we're lucky,

09:39.280 --> 09:40.680
we can start to see the rest of these

09:40.680 --> 09:42.800
start to follow to the downside and roll over

09:42.800 --> 09:44.040
and then happy days, right?

09:44.080 --> 09:45.960
But for now, that is not what we've got.

09:46.260 --> 09:49.300
And so I continue to accept the base case hypothesis here as well,

09:49.340 --> 09:52.380
which is that we will continue to see labour market deterioration

09:52.380 --> 09:55.060
before ultimately a big spike as we enter that recessionary period,

09:55.060 --> 09:58.140
likely coupled with the stock market completing a blow-off top

09:58.140 --> 10:00.080
and then crashing down into its four-year cycle low.

10:00.440 --> 10:02.440
And another one of these heavily contested calls of mine

10:02.440 --> 10:05.580
is that the rate hike thing indeed was just a narrative, right?

10:05.600 --> 10:07.080
And that we weren't going to see rate hikes

10:07.080 --> 10:09.360
and that the policy is already overly restrictive.

10:09.660 --> 10:12.040
And of course, this month, we managed to confirm that.

10:12.320 --> 10:13.880
The Fed held interest rates

10:13.880 --> 10:16.760
and we get to just restart this entire debate all over again.

10:16.860 --> 10:18.080
Right now, a bunch of people are saying,

10:18.160 --> 10:19.360
oh, they're going to hike at the next meeting.

10:19.360 --> 10:20.260
We need to see a hike.

10:20.360 --> 10:22.100
But I am here to suggest that's not going to happen.

10:22.220 --> 10:23.460
This will reprice in this direction

10:23.460 --> 10:24.480
and we'll see another pause.

10:24.580 --> 10:27.180
I ultimately believe we're far more likely to see cuts

10:27.180 --> 10:29.620
throughout the rest of this year than we are to see hikes.

10:29.800 --> 10:31.340
Again, I'm open to being proven wrong.

10:31.460 --> 10:33.660
I will pivot as new data presents, if it presents.

10:33.780 --> 10:35.480
But as of right now, it has not presented.

10:35.680 --> 10:37.900
And thus, I continue to accept this hypothesis of mine.

10:38.060 --> 10:41.020
And again, despite that big re-acceleration narrative out there

10:41.020 --> 10:43.340
or extended cycles or five-year cycles

10:43.340 --> 10:44.560
or whatever you want to call it,

10:44.820 --> 10:47.600
the truth is they keep quietly revising down the GDP data.

10:47.600 --> 10:49.080
It's not just the jobs data.

10:49.220 --> 10:50.740
It's also the GDP data now.

10:50.860 --> 10:52.600
The print of 1.4 wasn't very good.

10:52.600 --> 10:54.760
And then it was 0.7 and then 0.5.

10:54.880 --> 10:56.280
And again, it does not take a genius

10:56.280 --> 10:59.120
to look at the annualised real GDP growth here

10:59.120 --> 11:01.120
and see that this is, again, a downtrend.

11:01.280 --> 11:03.180
So based on this, we're actually not that far

11:03.180 --> 11:06.000
from entering a technical recession, at least by my books.

11:06.100 --> 11:07.500
And again, when we'd be able to go back

11:07.500 --> 11:08.920
and look at this data in hindsight,

11:09.100 --> 11:10.520
I don't think it would be a huge surprise

11:10.520 --> 11:12.000
to most people in hindsight.

11:12.300 --> 11:14.300
With that said, it all sounds doom and gloom.

11:14.340 --> 11:15.140
And I understand that.

11:15.180 --> 11:16.280
But it's not doom and gloom, OK?

11:16.300 --> 11:17.640
We are still very much long and strong.

11:17.700 --> 11:19.960
We're still very much optimistic on the stock market

11:19.960 --> 11:20.980
in at least the short term.

11:20.980 --> 11:22.780
We've even been building new exposure this week

11:22.780 --> 11:23.900
in the level three members section

11:23.900 --> 11:25.240
because, as I said right at the start,

11:25.260 --> 11:27.040
I'm not really interested in trying to call the top.

11:27.160 --> 11:29.440
I am far more interested in staying on trend

11:29.440 --> 11:31.940
and trying to capture as much from the market as possible,

11:31.940 --> 11:33.960
which brings me on to these alarm signals, right?

11:34.000 --> 11:36.500
When the high-yield credit spreads blow out like this,

11:36.560 --> 11:37.800
it tends to be synonymous

11:37.800 --> 11:39.240
with financially stressed conditions.

11:39.480 --> 11:40.600
We have worked our way back up

11:40.600 --> 11:41.640
to the edge of this range here,

11:41.700 --> 11:43.800
although the historic range I've been tracking is this one.

11:43.940 --> 11:44.740
But as of right now,

11:44.980 --> 11:46.160
the stock market is trying to bottom

11:46.160 --> 11:47.080
for a daily cycle low,

11:47.120 --> 11:48.640
as I'll show you when we get to the charts in a minute.

11:48.640 --> 11:50.540
And this is completely absent of challenging

11:50.540 --> 11:51.960
even the upper bound of the range,

11:52.160 --> 11:53.140
let alone breaking out.

11:53.220 --> 11:55.180
So I don't see any reason to run for the heels yet.

11:55.360 --> 11:57.700
Normally, when you start to get a daily cycle low

11:57.700 --> 11:59.800
that then comes up and fails that daily cycle,

11:59.960 --> 12:01.060
coupled with breakdowns

12:01.060 --> 12:03.060
and the high-yield credit spreads blow out,

12:03.220 --> 12:04.840
that for me is an alarm signal.

12:04.920 --> 12:05.760
That for me is a signal

12:05.760 --> 12:07.640
to get super defensive super fast.

12:07.820 --> 12:09.520
But right now, we just don't have that, OK?

12:09.560 --> 12:11.100
Right now, this says to me,

12:11.340 --> 12:12.700
there is gas left in the goose

12:12.700 --> 12:14.300
and we're going to run this thing hotter for longer.

12:14.460 --> 12:15.540
So again, I'm here for it,

12:15.560 --> 12:16.380
as you're going to see in a minute.

12:16.380 --> 12:19.060
And a very similar concept applies for the yield curves.

12:19.140 --> 12:20.820
When these things sweep to the upside,

12:20.980 --> 12:22.380
such as it did in the C19 era,

12:22.780 --> 12:24.800
the GFC and the dot-com bubble burst,

12:25.020 --> 12:26.240
it tends to be, for me,

12:26.500 --> 12:28.440
a warning sign that it's time to get defensive,

12:28.640 --> 12:30.120
especially when coupled with breakdowns

12:30.120 --> 12:31.560
and failed cycles in the stock market.

12:31.700 --> 12:32.520
But as it stands right now,

12:32.600 --> 12:34.300
this does not look at the hard right edge

12:34.300 --> 12:35.820
anything like that at all, does it?

12:35.820 --> 12:38.380
This does not look like an accelerative sweep.

12:38.560 --> 12:40.140
